{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Oregon and California Railroad", "line": "Oregon and California: Portland \u2013 Ashland", "id": "oc-portland-ashland-hornbrook", "color": "#3d5c3a", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 40, "speed_source": "estimated/blended: fast, flat running through the Willamette Valley bracketed by slow, sharply curved mountain grades through Cow Creek Canyon (Roseburg-Grants Pass) and the Siskiyou Summit switchbacks (Ashland-Hornbrook); unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1868, "end": 1872, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Transportation tycoon Ben Holladay's Oregon and California Railroad broke ground in Portland in spring 1868 and built south, largely with Chinese laborers: Oregon City (1869), Salem (Sept 27 1870), Albany (Dec 25 1870), Eugene (Oct 15 1871), and Roseburg (Dec 3 1872). Construction then stalled for nearly nine years for lack of funds. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_and_California_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1881, "end": 1887, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Work resumed in 1881 under new ownership, reaching Myrtle Creek Aug 14 1882, Grants Pass Dec 2 1883, and Ashland May 4 1884, following Cow Creek Canyon's notoriously twisting grade south of Roseburg. The California-based Southern Pacific Company took control of the O&C during this final push. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_and_California_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1887, "end": 1924, "headway_min": 720, "note": "On Dec 17 1887 Southern Pacific president Charles Crocker drove the last spike at Ashland, joining crews building south over the Siskiyou Summit (4,133 ft, an 18-tunnel crossing including a 3,108-foot summit bore) with Southern Pacific's own line built north from Redding -- completing the first-ever Portland-San Francisco rail connection, marketed as the Shasta Route. The Oregon & California Express (trains 15/16, Oakland-Portland) launched the same day, briefly renamed the Shasta Limited 1895-97, and made its last run in November 1924 as part of a broader SP service restructuring.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_and_California_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1924, "end": 1926, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Some secondary or local passenger service likely continued over the Siskiyou Line after the Express's 1924 discontinuation, though a specific successor train is not documented here; treated as reduced/local-only service pending better sourcing.", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_and_California_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1926, "end": 1958, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Southern Pacific completed the lower-grade Cascade Line via Klamath Falls in 1926 (see southern_pacific_cascade.geojson), which became the primary Portland-San Francisco route; the steep, curvy Siskiyou Line settled into secondary status, retaining local and freight service. A specific end date for through passenger service on this exact routing isn't well documented; 1958 is used here as a placeholder for the late-1950s decline of secondary rail passenger service nationally and should be treated as approximate, not sourced. SP sold the Ashland-Eugene 'Siskiyou Line' portion on Dec 31 1994 to what's now the Central Oregon and Pacific Railroad (a Genesee & Wyoming shortline), which still hosts freight today over an on-again, off-again history: closed 2008, partially reopened Ashland-Weed CA in Nov 2015 after a $13 million repair project, and has faced further closures since.", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_and_California_Railroad"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.96379, 42.42088], "to": [-122.81668, 42.26599], "chord_mi": 13.02, "traced_mi": 13.08, "snap_m": [1, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Siskiyou Subdivision (13.08 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-123.1081, 44.6224], "to": [-123.10821, 44.32881], "chord_mi": 20.29, "traced_mi": 20.29, "snap_m": [1, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Brooklyn Subdivision (20.29 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-122.76907, 45.21353], "to": [-122.93127, 45.08189], "chord_mi": 12.23, "traced_mi": 12.05, "snap_m": [0, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Brooklyn Subdivision (12.05 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 13 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed). Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 3 ruled segments totalling 46 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[